{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-351200",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-9 & =A(x-3)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=3$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-9 & =A(3-3)+B \\\\ -3 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-9 & =A(0-3)-3 \\\\ -9 & =-3A-3 \\\\ -3A & =-6 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-3}-\\frac{3}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
